# Content-addressed inputs must produce the same bytes on every machine.
#
# `fixtures/trials/**` is pinned by `scripts/seed-content.mjs`, whose keccak digest is posted
# on-chain as a trial's spec/tests hash. With `core.autocrlf=true` (the default on Windows
# developer boxes, including this repository's) a checkout handed those files CRLF line endings
# while the committed blob stayed LF - so the same commit produced two different digests
# depending on the machine that read the file. A digest posted from one box would not resolve to
# content on another, which is the one thing a commitment must never do.
#
# `text eol=lf` makes the working tree byte-identical to the blob. Scoped to the fixtures on
# purpose: this is only where a file's bytes become a hash somebody else has to reproduce.
fixtures/** text eol=lf

# The edge runs on Linux. A CRLF in a shell script is a runtime error there
# ("\r: command not found"), and a CRLF in a Caddyfile or an nginx map is a
# parse error. Pin these to LF so the line ending cannot become a production
# incident on the way to the box.
deploy/edge/** text eol=lf

# Generated artifacts are compared byte-for-byte by the gate that guards them.
#
# `scripts/params-codegen.mjs --check` regenerates in memory and diffs against the file on disk;
# the generated text is a template literal in that file, so its newlines are the source file's own,
# while a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` smudges the committed blob to whatever it likes.
# Measured 2026-10-24: a clean checkout of a commit whose values matched the JSON exactly reported
# drift on all three artifacts. A byte compare there guards the line ending, not the numbers — and
# teaches everyone to distrust the gate that exists to catch a parameter hand-edited into a
# generated file.
#
# What this pin does and does not do, stated plainly because the difference matters: it makes the
# clean filter strip CRLF from these paths on every future commit, and `eol=lf` stops CR being
# re-inserted at checkout. It does NOT rewrite the blobs already in history, three of which were
# committed with CRLF inside them, so a checkout of the current commit still shows them. That is why
# the gate compares with line endings folded, and why the fold is the load-bearing half.
params/generated/** text eol=lf
crucible-contracts/contracts/Params.sol text eol=lf
packages/smith-sdk/src/params.ts text eol=lf
# The funder entrypoint is executed by /bin/sh inside a Linux image; a CRLF shebang fails.
apps/funder/docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
